WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.680
Welcome to Day 1 of Case Management Training with Legal Support Help.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:09.272
All week we work one file, John Doe versus Robert Smith,

00:00:09.272 --> 00:00:13.211
a passenger injury from a collision on February 6th,

00:00:13.211 --> 00:00:13.590
2026.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.740
Today we open that file.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:21.392
We look at what a Case Manager actually owns, how a case moves from the first call

00:00:21.392 --> 00:00:25.530
to resolution, and how you keep it clean, compliant and on time.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:31.080
Keep John's file in mind as we go, because every idea on these slides lands on it.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.630
Today has three parts.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.780
First, the fundamentals: what case management is and what you are responsible for.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:45.587
Second, intake, case evaluation and the decision to accept John Doe's case,

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:49.330
including the conflict check and the coverage behind the claim.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.003
Third, the Treatment Phase, where most of a personal injury case's value is built or

00:00:55.003 --> 00:00:55.320
lost.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:01.510
On our file, that is where the fourteen-day gap sits, so pay close attention there.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.899
Case management is the coordination, tracking and handling of a case from intake to

00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:09.540
resolution.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.770
The slide gives three tests, and every task on a file should pass all of them.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.000
Timely progression means nothing sits without a next step and a date.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:24.030
Documentation and compliance means that if it isn't in the file, it didn't happen.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.471
And communication means the client, Atty. Thorne,

00:01:27.471 --> 00:01:31.004
the carriers and the providers each know what they need,

00:01:31.004 --> 00:01:32.140
when they need it.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.901
On John's file, those parties are already lining up:

00:01:35.901 --> 00:01:39.311
Keystone, Harbor Point, Cardinal State, Peach State,

00:01:39.311 --> 00:01:41.410
and a growing list of providers.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.520
Your job is to keep all of them moving in step.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:52.575
These seven stages are the life of a file, from intake and opening through planning,

00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:56.376
documentation, communication, monitoring, billing support,

00:01:56.376 --> 00:01:58.670
and finally resolution and closing.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.860
The point of the arrows is that nothing gets skipped.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.340
A file that jumps from intake straight to treatment,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.650
without a plan or a coverage picture, will stall later.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.721
John Doe's file opened with the intake call on February 9th,

00:02:12.721 --> 00:02:17.000
2026, and it will travel through every one of these stages this week.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.410
Atty. Thorne makes the legal decisions along the way,

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.510
but you are the one constant who carries the file from start to finish.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.020
Here are the duties that fill a Case Manager's day.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.370
Notice how practical they are.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.640
Case updates and summaries keep Atty. Thorne current without him reading every page.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:41.391
Compliance monitoring means you know every deadline,

00:02:41.391 --> 00:02:46.623
starting with the statute of limitations, docketed on our file for February 6th,

00:02:46.623 --> 00:02:46.950
2028.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.628
Case health analysis means reading the whole file for risk,

00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:53.900
the way you would spot John's fourteen-day gap on day one.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.730
And the last duty matters most.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.120
You plan and you recommend.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:03.790
Decisions of legal strategy, value and settlement belong to the attorney.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.933
A case moves through structured phases, and each one has its own goals and

00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:11.740
deliverables.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.326
Intake; investigation, which we also call claim set up;

00:03:16.326 --> 00:03:21.177
treatment; pre-demand; demand; settlement and lien negotiation;

00:03:21.177 --> 00:03:25.490
disbursement; and litigation when a case doesn't settle.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.880
Knowing the phase tells you what the file needs next.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.350
Today we cover the first three.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.279
On John's file, that runs from the intake call on February 9th,

00:03:36.279 --> 00:03:41.293
2026, through claim set up with the carriers, and into his treatment,

00:03:41.293 --> 00:03:46.817
which ends when Dr. Reyes declares maximum medical improvement on June 12th,

00:03:46.817 --> 00:03:47.180
2026.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:54.650
Intake is where you take control of the file, and it is much more than data entry.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Everything later depends on it.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:02.280
A wrong date of loss, a missed party or a skipped conflict check will follow the

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.190
case all the way to the demand.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.080
The slide lists four jobs: capture complete client details,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.440
screen for conflicts, assess whether the case is viable,

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.660
and build a properly structured file.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.586
On our file, that means John Doe's date of birth,

00:04:17.586 --> 00:04:22.623
August 14th, 1991, his address at 2214 Maple Hollow Road in Riverton,

00:04:22.623 --> 00:04:28.170
his phone and email, the date of loss, and the fact that he was a passenger.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.320
This is also the phase that decides whether the case moves forward at all.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.390
Here is intake as a real workflow.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.460
You talk with the client or review what they sent.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:45.890
You ask the structured questions: who, what, when, where and how.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.320
You run the conflict check before anything else moves.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:53.990
You review the documents, and you build the file with the firm's naming convention.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.875
On our file, the intake call was Monday, February 9th,

00:04:57.875 --> 00:05:03.111
2026, and John signed the retainer and the HIPAA authorization in the office the

00:05:03.111 --> 00:05:03.700
same day.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:10.170
The file number is L S H 2026 M V A 770412.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.917
The conflict check covers every name on the file,

00:05:13.917 --> 00:05:17.661
not only the defendant: Robert Smith, Keystone Mutual,

00:05:17.661 --> 00:05:21.059
the host driver Kevin Alvarez, and John's mother,

00:05:21.059 --> 00:05:21.960
Margaret Doe.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:25.870
Then the case is approved or escalated, and the file is logged.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:30.585
You should be able to run that sequence on your own and tell the client clearly what

00:05:30.585 --> 00:05:31.260
happens next.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.920
Initial client contact gathers the basic facts,

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:40.890
judges urgency, and decides whether the case can go forward.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.880
Your role as Case Manager starts once intake is done.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:48.390
You are the quality check before a case becomes active.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.860
Read the intake the way an adjuster eventually will.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.282
On John's file, one fact should stop you at once:

00:05:55.282 --> 00:06:00.359
the crash was February 6th, and he had not yet been treated when he called on the

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:00.610
9th.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.040
His first treatment didn't come until February 20th.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:08.630
That fourteen-day gap is flagged at intake, not discovered at the demand.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:12.259
Being the gatekeeper means you catch it, document it,

00:06:12.259 --> 00:06:14.300
and make sure Atty. Thorne knows.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:21.650
After the verification call confirms the facts of loss, the question changes.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.720
You are no longer gathering information.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:29.260
You are checking whether the case meets the firm's criteria for acceptance,

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.830
based on verified facts and a complete intake.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:36.777
On our file, the facts hold up: John was a restrained front-seat passenger,

00:06:36.777 --> 00:06:40.822
the Riverton police report cites Robert Smith for failure to stop,

00:06:40.822 --> 00:06:44.500
and there is a recovery stack of insurance behind the claim.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.690
The risk is also clear: the treatment gap.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.000
The Case Manager presents both sides in the file,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.840
and Atty. Thorne makes the acceptance decision.

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:59.973
Intake bottlenecks are the places where a file slows down,

00:06:59.973 --> 00:07:01.950
gets stuck or goes inconsistent.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.713
The slide names five common causes: missing information,

00:07:05.713 --> 00:07:08.944
slow client responses, poorly documented submissions,

00:07:08.944 --> 00:07:13.820
delays in verification or the conflict check, and unclear eligibility decisions.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:19.423
They matter because every day lost at intake is a day lost against the statute of

00:07:19.423 --> 00:07:22.730
limitations, and a day the client may go untreated.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.238
Over the next few slides we take each one in turn,

00:07:26.238 --> 00:07:29.080
and see where it shows up on John Doe's file.

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:34.355
The first bottleneck is incomplete information:

00:07:34.355 --> 00:07:38.239
missing contact details or incident facts, no supporting documents,

00:07:38.239 --> 00:07:39.630
or a vague case summary.

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:43.460
Each one sends you back to the client before the file can move.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.610
On our file, some blanks are normal on day one.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.542
Cardinal State hasn't assigned an adjuster yet,

00:07:49.542 --> 00:07:54.047
and Keystone gave its $10,000 limit verbally but won't confirm it in writing until

00:07:54.047 --> 00:07:55.640
it has signed authorizations.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.187
You record exactly what is known and what is pending,

00:07:59.187 --> 00:08:03.590
and you set a follow-up for each gap, so nothing stays blank by accident.

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:09.140
The second bottleneck is the client who goes quiet.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:14.135
Clarification requests go unanswered, confirmations never come back,

00:08:14.135 --> 00:08:15.570
documents trickle in.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.800
John Doe's file shows this in its first week.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:23.190
The last logged contact was February 10th, 2026.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:26.787
Calls followed on the 11th, the 13th and the 16th,

00:08:26.787 --> 00:08:29.904
then written warnings, and a call to his mother,

00:08:29.904 --> 00:08:34.060
Margaret, as the emergency contact, with no case details shared.

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:38.820
One lesson from the file: the first two voicemails were left but never logged,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.290
and the system flagged it.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:43.880
An attempt you don't log is an attempt you can't prove.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:49.390
John finally called back on February 23rd, after the Final Warning Letter went out.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:55.675
The third bottleneck is the conflict check: system backlogs,

00:08:55.675 --> 00:08:59.660
incomplete entries, or unclear information about the other side.

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:01.450
The rule is simple.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.800
The conflict check runs before anything else moves, and it runs on complete names.

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:12.624
On our file, the defendant is Robert Smith, with an address on the police report of

00:09:12.624 --> 00:09:14.470
118 Larkspur Lane, Riverton.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:19.146
But the check also covers the carriers and the people around the claim,

00:09:19.146 --> 00:09:22.740
including Kevin Alvarez, the host driver, and Margaret Doe.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:27.170
A conflict check run on half the information is not a conflict check.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.564
The fourth bottleneck is the intake form error:

00:09:32.564 --> 00:09:37.103
wrong dates, inconsistent statements, missing signatures or authorizations,

00:09:37.103 --> 00:09:39.160
and duplicate or misfiled records.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:43.190
These are cheap to fix at intake and expensive to fix later.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:45.300
Our file proves it.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:50.368
Months later, Summit Imaging's MRI history gave the injury date as February 16th

00:09:50.368 --> 00:09:54.850
instead of February 6th, and it took a written correction request to fix it.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.584
So at intake, confirm the date of loss, February 6th,

00:09:58.584 --> 00:10:03.246
2026, against the police report, and make sure the retainer and the HIPAA

00:10:03.246 --> 00:10:05.800
authorization are both signed and filed.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.258
The fifth bottleneck is eligibility uncertainty:

00:10:11.258 --> 00:10:15.862
an intake with no clear legal assessment, a case that needs attorney review,

00:10:15.862 --> 00:10:18.830
or facts of loss or liability that are ambiguous.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:23.140
When eligibility is unclear, don't guess and don't sit on it.

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:26.810
Escalate the file to the attorney and document why.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:32.908
On John's file, liability looks strong, but the fourteen-day gap is a real question

00:10:32.908 --> 00:10:33.600
for value.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.790
That isn't yours to settle.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:40.700
Write it down plainly, attach what supports it, and let Atty. Thorne decide.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:48.117
In a legal setting, case management moves from best effort to strict compliance,

00:10:48.117 --> 00:10:51.889
and your tools make that possible: the case management system,

00:10:51.889 --> 00:10:56.330
task trackers and calendars, document management and workflow automation.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:58.960
The strategy behind them is simple.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:03.632
Every case is a race against the statute of limitations and against the gap in

00:11:03.632 --> 00:11:04.630
treatment defense.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:10.606
On John's file, both are already on the board: the SOL docketed for February 6th,

00:11:10.606 --> 00:11:12.620
2028, and a fourteen-day gap.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:16.570
In intake and investigation the focus is liability and coverage.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:21.633
Here that means Harbor Point's P I P on the Escape,

00:11:21.633 --> 00:11:26.161
the BI and both UM layers, and the police report,

00:11:26.161 --> 00:11:27.640
R P D 26 018842.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.965
It also means preserving Margaret's doorbell video,

00:11:30.965 --> 00:11:33.590
which John was told to keep on February 17th.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:38.134
In treatment the focus is damages: compliance, no further gaps,

00:11:38.134 --> 00:11:43.527
and early imaging, which for John was the cervical MRI at Summit Imaging on March

00:11:43.527 --> 00:11:43.860
13th.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:48.930
In the demand phase it is value: the specials and a final narrative.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.920
And in litigation, it is evidence: discovery, depositions and experts.

00:11:56.470 --> 00:12:01.386
The last bottleneck is the communication gap: intake not updating the Case Manager,

00:12:01.386 --> 00:12:05.710
a packet that arrives incomplete, or departments talking past each other.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.569
On a file like John's, many hands touch it early:

00:12:09.569 --> 00:12:13.580
the intake team, the carriers, the clinic, Atty. Thorne.

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.490
Closing the gaps is part of your job.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:21.691
Confirm you have the complete intake packet, log every hand-off with the date and

00:12:21.691 --> 00:12:26.722
the person, and if something is missing, ask for it the same day rather than waiting

00:12:26.722 --> 00:12:27.800
for it to surface.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.070
Now claim set up, the early evaluation.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:35.580
It rests on two questions.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:40.810
Coverage: which policies apply, what type, what limits, and what exclusions.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:44.513
Liability: who caused the crash, whether fault is clear,

00:12:44.513 --> 00:12:49.242
shared or disputed, what evidence proves it, and whether comparative negligence

00:12:49.242 --> 00:12:50.200
comes into play.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:55.445
On our file, coverage takes real work, because John was a passenger and owns no car,

00:12:55.445 --> 00:12:57.310
so no policy of his own applies.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:01.980
Liability looks clean, but you still ask the comparative negligence question.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:06.496
The EMS run report records him as a restrained front-seat passenger,

00:13:06.496 --> 00:13:11.090
and you keep that record, because the defense may later argue the opposite.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:18.720
Case projection is the early estimate of value, risk and how the case will move.

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:22.629
It draws on coverage, liability strength, injury severity,

00:13:22.629 --> 00:13:25.390
expected treatment and documentation quality.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.780
Each piece answers a question.

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.810
Coverage asks whether there is money to pay.

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:33.760
Liability asks who is responsible.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:37.910
Projection asks what the case is likely worth and how it will move.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:42.347
On John's file, the coverage ceiling is a $40,000 stack,

00:13:42.347 --> 00:13:47.820
liability is strong, and the fourteen-day gap is the obvious risk to value.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:51.090
Remember that value is Atty. Thorne's call.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:55.520
Your projection supports his decision; it is never a promise to the client.

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:01.310
Now let's apply this to John Doe versus Robert Smith.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:06.060
John was the front-seat passenger in Kevin Alvarez's 2020 Ford Escape.

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:09.967
Robert Smith, driving a 2017 Chevrolet Silverado,

00:14:09.967 --> 00:14:15.992
ran the all-way stop at Maple Hollow Road and Birch Avenue and struck the passenger

00:14:15.992 --> 00:14:20.130
door on February 6th, 2026, at about 7:10 in the morning.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.640
The coverage stack pays in order.

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:28.349
First, Smith's BI with Keystone Mutual, claim K M 26 440871,

00:14:28.349 --> 00:14:34.870
a $10,000 limit, which Keystone gave verbally and you confirm in writing.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.412
Second, Host UM with Harbor Point, the Escape's policy,

00:14:39.412 --> 00:14:43.300
$20,000, which opens only once the BI is exhausted.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:47.860
Third, Relative UM with Cardinal State Mutual, $10,000,

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:51.610
because John lives with his mother, Margaret Doe.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.440
That is a $40,000 stack.

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:59.190
Margaret's Bluewater policy rejected UM in writing, so it adds no layer.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:04.476
The key insight: never settle or release the BI claim without the UM carriers'

00:15:04.476 --> 00:15:08.020
written consent, because doing so can forfeit the stack.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:11.810
Now liability.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:16.160
This was a side-impact collision at an all-way stop in Riverton.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:20.310
Kevin Alvarez stopped eastbound on Maple Hollow Road and proceeded.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.793
Robert Smith, northbound on Birch Avenue, didn't stop,

00:15:23.793 --> 00:15:27.275
and hit the front passenger door at about 28 miles per hour,

00:15:27.275 --> 00:15:30.060
pushing the Escape into Margaret's parked Civic.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:34.862
The evidence is unusually good: the Riverton police report,

00:15:34.862 --> 00:15:38.634
R P D 26 018842, citing Smith for failure to stop;

00:15:38.634 --> 00:15:43.538
an independent witness, Ruth Calloway, of 2209 Maple Hollow Road;

00:15:43.538 --> 00:15:46.330
and Margaret's doorbell-camera video.

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:50.040
John was a passenger, so none of the driving was his.

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:54.823
Your job is to check each piece against the police report and make sure the video is

00:15:54.823 --> 00:15:56.670
preserved before it is overwritten.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:00.420
And remember, fault and value are Atty. Thorne's call.

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:04.010
You document the facts; you never predict the outcome.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:10.480
Before clinical planning begins, clear the Regulatory Triple-Check.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:12.230
First, HIPAA.

00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:17.436
A general release isn't enough; you need a claim-specific authorization that names

00:16:17.436 --> 00:16:21.700
the date of loss and lets you talk with the legal and insurance parties.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:26.410
John signed his at intake on February 9th, 2026.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.360
Second, Medicare and Medicaid reporting.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:36.462
If the client is 65 or older or on SSDI, the case is reported to the BCRC under the

00:16:36.462 --> 00:16:39.390
MMSEA, or the settlement can freeze.

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:43.484
John is thirty-four and working, but you confirm it and document it;

00:16:43.484 --> 00:16:44.420
you never assume.

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:47.410
Third, subrogation and payer order.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:52.354
On our file, Harbor Point's P I P pays first, then Harbor Point's MedPay,

00:16:52.354 --> 00:16:57.200
then Peach State Health Plan, which asserted its reimbursement right on first

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:01.480
notice, then the Letter of Protection with Riverton Spine and Rehab.

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:07.031
The case plan is often due within 30 to 60 days,

00:17:07.031 --> 00:17:10.470
depending on the state, and it stands on three pillars.

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:13.100
Clinical covers the interventions.

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:18.439
For John, that is chiropractic and physical therapy at Riverton Spine and Rehab,

00:17:18.439 --> 00:17:23.365
two to three visits a week for twelve weeks, and an orthopedic consult with Dr.

00:17:23.365 --> 00:17:25.610
Alana Reyes at Lakeside Orthopedics.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:28.520
Functional covers daily life and work.

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:32.444
John is a warehouse shift lead at Northgate Distribution,

00:17:32.444 --> 00:17:35.390
lifting up to 50 pounds and driving a forklift,

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:37.710
so his return to work status matters.

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:41.260
And subrogation is the payer order we just covered.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:45.139
The fourteen-day gap also goes into the plan's risks band,

00:17:45.139 --> 00:17:47.330
written down plainly from the start.

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:53.280
Your file is discoverable in litigation, so keep it clean.

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:55.390
Three things to track.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:59.949
Record integrity: your system should be SOC2 compliant,

00:17:59.949 --> 00:18:02.780
with an audit trail no one can alter.

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:07.389
State-specific timelines: in no-fault states like New York or Florida,

00:18:07.389 --> 00:18:10.370
the plan must schedule independent medical exams.

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:12.640
That is the general rule.

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:16.288
Riverton is an add-on P I P state, not no-fault,

00:18:16.288 --> 00:18:19.929
but it does have an Emergency Medical Condition rule:

00:18:19.929 --> 00:18:24.670
without a physician's finding, P I P medical benefits stop at $2,500.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:28.220
So know which rules your file actually runs under.

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:30.810
And Letter of Protection management.

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:36.353
John has Peach State coverage, but Riverton Spine and Rehab is out of network,

00:18:36.353 --> 00:18:40.147
so his care there runs under an LOP signed February 23rd,

00:18:40.147 --> 00:18:40.480
2026.

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:46.070
Track every visit and every charge on it, so no bill surprises anyone at the end.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:52.460
The golden rule of claim set up is coordinate, don't commingle.

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:57.570
The medical plan and the legal claim run on two parallel tracks that never cross.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.625
Never write a legal opinion into a medical note,

00:19:00.625 --> 00:19:04.120
because defense counsel will use it to attack your neutrality.

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:08.898
On John's file, a note that says the client attended his Riverton Spine and Rehab

00:19:08.898 --> 00:19:11.030
visit and reported neck pain is a fact.

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:14.554
A note that says the gap won't hurt the case is an opinion,

00:19:14.554 --> 00:19:16.060
and it doesn't belong there.

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:19.930
Keep your file clean, because everything in it can be discovered.

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:24.080
Now the Treatment Phase.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:28.510
The question changes from what does the client need, to is the plan working.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:31.380
Your role rests on three pillars.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.959
Facilitation means the client can actually reach care:

00:19:34.959 --> 00:19:37.410
transportation, paperwork and scheduling.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:42.029
Coordination means the chiropractor, the therapist and the doctor aren't working at

00:19:42.029 --> 00:19:42.800
cross-purposes.

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:46.310
Advocacy means stepping in when the client hits a wall.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:49.220
On John's file, all three are tested.

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:53.482
He has no car of his own, he is seeing several providers at once,

00:19:53.482 --> 00:19:56.010
and his care is paid from several sources.

00:19:56.360 --> 00:20:00.097
This phase is the engine room of the case, and you balance support,

00:20:00.097 --> 00:20:02.440
monitoring and problem-solving throughout.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:07.750
Your responsibilities here come in three parts.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:12.983
Monitoring progress means looking at real clinical and functional outcomes,

00:20:12.983 --> 00:20:14.220
not checking boxes.

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:19.297
At a check-in, ask whether John made his Tuesday visit and whether the numbness in

00:20:19.297 --> 00:20:22.410
his right hand has changed, not just how he's feeling.

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:26.240
Then document in GIRP or SOAP format.

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:29.510
Identifying barriers means being a detective.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:33.112
Internal barriers include motivation and mental health;

00:20:33.112 --> 00:20:36.660
external ones include transportation and insurance problems.

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:40.618
And crisis intervention means that when something urgent comes up,

00:20:40.618 --> 00:20:43.570
you deal with it first, and adjust the plan around it.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:49.200
Three communication skills keep a client on track.

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:53.775
Motivational interviewing keeps commitment going after the newness of treatment

00:20:53.775 --> 00:20:54.310
wears off.

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:58.607
Boundaries protect you from burnout and keep the client moving toward

00:20:58.607 --> 00:20:59.580
self-sufficiency.

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:04.475
Active listening makes the client feel heard and reduces misunderstandings with

00:21:04.475 --> 00:21:05.050
providers.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:08.840
And remember, treatment is a cycle, not a straight line.

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:12.910
Assess the response, evaluate the plan, and adjust.

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:16.393
On John's file, the plan changes more than once:

00:21:16.393 --> 00:21:20.308
Dr. Reyes orders an MRI, then refers him to pain management,

00:21:20.308 --> 00:21:21.940
then counseling is added.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:26.970
When a client isn't reaching their goals, look at the plan first, not the person.

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:33.405
Your notes are the legal and professional record of treatment,

00:21:33.405 --> 00:21:35.160
so structure them with GIRP.

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:38.590
Goal: which part of the plan are we working on today.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:40.980
Intervention: what you did.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:45.610
Response: how the client reacted, in his own words where you can.

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.480
Plan: the next step and its date.

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:53.190
Midway through treatment, many clients plateau as the early energy fades.

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:55.900
That's where motivational interviewing helps.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:22:00.855
Develop discrepancy, by showing the gap between what the client is doing and what he

00:22:00.855 --> 00:22:04.090
wants, such as John getting back to full duty at Northgate.

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:07.640
Roll with resistance: don't argue; explore why.

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:13.150
And use OARS: open questions, affirmations, reflective listening and summaries.

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:19.980
You are the hub of the wheel, and the key skill is the warm handoff.

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:23.595
Instead of handing a client a phone number, you say:

00:22:23.595 --> 00:22:26.170
let's call them together and book it now.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:30.160
That stops the referral leak, where clients drop out between providers.

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:35.212
On John's file there are several transitions: from urgent care to the emergency

00:22:35.212 --> 00:22:39.081
department at Riverton General, then to Riverton Spine and Rehab,

00:22:39.081 --> 00:22:42.950
Lakeside Orthopedics, Summit Imaging and Crescent Pain and Spine.

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:45.700
Each is a point where he could slip.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:48.709
Then manage boundaries and avoid mission creep,

00:22:48.709 --> 00:22:51.650
where every new problem pulls you off the main goal.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:55.120
Coach the client rather than solving everything yourself.

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:59.961
And end every contact with one question: what is the one thing that might get in the

00:22:59.961 --> 00:23:00.710
way this week?

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:06.540
Know the standard treatment protocols and their red flags.

00:23:06.890 --> 00:23:09.770
Physical therapy is usually the baseline of care.

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:15.141
Red flags are more than twelve sessions with no improvement and no referral or

00:23:15.141 --> 00:23:20.484
imaging, passive care with no active exercise, and two or more missed appointments,

00:23:20.484 --> 00:23:23.960
which lets the defense argue the injury isn't serious.

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:27.070
On our file, that last one is real.

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:31.075
In April, John missed visits on the 3rd, the 7th,

00:23:31.075 --> 00:23:34.580
the 9th and the 14th, and you need to know why.

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:40.339
For surgery, the red flags are about safety: surgery before therapy or injections

00:23:40.339 --> 00:23:44.413
outside an emergency, left and right switched in the records,

00:23:44.413 --> 00:23:47.619
a fever or redness the client is told to ignore,

00:23:47.619 --> 00:23:50.290
and sharp mechanical pain after surgery.

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:54.170
John's file has no surgery; his care stayed conservative,

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:56.400
with one epidural steroid injection.

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:03.975
Pain management is the most scrutinized area, because of opioid rules and medical

00:24:03.975 --> 00:24:05.030
necessity audits.

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:08.403
The red flags on the slide are general: an opioid,

00:24:08.403 --> 00:24:11.608
a muscle relaxant and a sedative prescribed together;

00:24:11.608 --> 00:24:15.659
a urine drug screen showing the client isn't taking his medication;

00:24:15.659 --> 00:24:18.380
and doses rising with no change in diagnosis.

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:24.964
On John's file, pain management means Dr. Omar Haddad at Crescent Pain and Spine,

00:24:24.964 --> 00:24:29.505
who gave a cervical epidural steroid injection on May 15th,

00:24:29.505 --> 00:24:29.890
2026.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:32.767
Traumatic brain injury isn't part of this file,

00:24:32.767 --> 00:24:37.229
but the red flag is worth knowing: symptoms that never appeared at the E R and then

00:24:37.229 --> 00:24:40.240
show up months later need medical validation right away.

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:44.270
And behavioral health has to be tied to the trauma of the accident.

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.340
John's counseling was about crash-related anxiety,

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:50.060
which is exactly the kind of record that holds up.

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:55.690
Before you build a treatment plan, you assess the whole person.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:59.640
Clinical: the physical, cognitive and psychological impairments.

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:03.070
Social: housing, family support and money.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.503
John lives with his mother, Margaret, and owns no car,

00:25:06.503 --> 00:25:08.900
which matters for getting to appointments.

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:12.690
Pre-morbid status: the baseline before the accident.

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:17.155
John had three chiropractic visits in 2021 for low-back stiffness,

00:25:17.155 --> 00:25:19.960
which resolved, and no prior neck complaints.

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:23.270
You find that history before the defense does.

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:26.460
And vocational: can he go back to his job?

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:32.184
John is a warehouse shift lead at Northgate, who started on January 12th,

00:25:32.184 --> 00:25:33.730
2026, at $22 an hour.

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:41.029
In a personal injury case, goals have to be defensible and measurable for clinicians

00:25:41.029 --> 00:25:42.160
and adjusters alike.

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:44.590
Use SMART goals.

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:49.460
The slide's example, walking 50 meters with a rolling walker, is general.

00:25:49.810 --> 00:25:54.978
For John, a specific goal is tied to his job, such as lifting 50 pounds at work

00:25:54.978 --> 00:25:55.370
again.

00:25:55.720 --> 00:26:01.451
Measurable means a standardized scale, such as a disability score or the PHQ-9 for

00:26:01.451 --> 00:26:01.800
mood.

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:05.430
Achievable means realistic for the prognosis.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:08.940
Relevant means tied to the neck injury in the claim.

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:14.544
And time-bound means a clear milestone, such as the twelve-week review in his plan

00:26:14.544 --> 00:26:16.850
of care at Riverton Spine and Rehab.

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:23.462
The care coordination strategy maps the interventions the client needs,

00:26:23.462 --> 00:26:24.320
in four groups.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:29.510
Acute and restorative care: surgery, physiotherapy and occupational therapy.

00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:33.615
On John's file, that's chiropractic and physical therapy,

00:26:33.615 --> 00:26:37.700
orthopedic care with Dr. Reyes, and the injection at Crescent.

00:26:38.050 --> 00:26:41.450
Psychological support: counseling for anxiety or trauma.

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:45.890
For John, that came through Northgate's employee assistance program,

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:49.680
six sessions with Renee Castillo at Stonebridge E A P Services.

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:53.727
Durable medical equipment: wheelchairs, ramps and assistive technology,

00:26:53.727 --> 00:26:55.550
which John's file doesn't call for.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:27:00.140
And rehabilitation milestones: the steps from one level of care to the next.

00:27:02.690 --> 00:27:07.633
This is the Standard Treatment Map, the typical path of care in a personal injury

00:27:07.633 --> 00:27:10.746
case: emergency services and the E R, then imaging,

00:27:10.746 --> 00:27:14.835
then providers like chiropractic, physical therapy and orthopedics,

00:27:14.835 --> 00:27:19.290
then pain management, and, where needed, surgery and post-operative care.

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:21.200
The map is general.

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:23.630
Now trace John's path on it.

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:27.540
EMS saw him at the scene and he declined transport.

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:31.465
His first treatment was Riverton Urgent Care on February 20th,

00:27:31.465 --> 00:27:35.904
and from there an ambulance took him to Riverton General Hospital's emergency

00:27:35.904 --> 00:27:38.210
department, where he was kept overnight.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:42.227
Then Riverton Spine and Rehab from February 24th,

00:27:42.227 --> 00:27:47.841
Lakeside Orthopedics on March 5th, the MRI at Summit Imaging on March 13th,

00:27:47.841 --> 00:27:50.760
and pain management at Crescent in May.

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:54.430
Learning the map lets you see when a client drifts off it.

00:27:56.980 --> 00:28:00.916
Mastering the map is the difference between watching a recovery and driving it,

00:28:00.916 --> 00:28:05.002
because departures from the map are what trigger insurance denials and independent

00:28:05.002 --> 00:28:05.700
medical exams.

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:10.813
Phase one, acute, days 1 to 14: the E R, imaging and early care,

00:28:10.813 --> 00:28:15.130
with your focus on causation and P I P or MedPay coverage.

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:19.547
On John's file, those fourteen days passed with no treatment at all,

00:28:19.547 --> 00:28:21.760
which is why the gap is so important.

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:28.288
Phase two, sub-acute, weeks 2 to 8: active therapy two or three times a week and

00:28:28.288 --> 00:28:29.910
specialist follow-up.

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:35.484
Phase three, restorative, months 2 to 6: injections and psychological adjustment,

00:28:35.484 --> 00:28:38.580
like John's injection in May and his counseling.

00:28:38.930 --> 00:28:43.711
Phase four, maintenance, ends with maximum medical improvement,

00:28:43.711 --> 00:28:47.050
which Dr. Reyes declared on June 12th, 2026.

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:54.320
When a client drifts off the map, find out why before the insurer stops paying.

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:56.790
There are three kinds of variance.

00:28:57.140 --> 00:29:01.579
Clinical variance is a new complication, like nerve symptoms that weren't in the

00:29:01.579 --> 00:29:02.300
first report.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:08.279
John's urgent-care note records numbness to his right hand since February 18th,

00:29:08.279 --> 00:29:11.770
and the MRI later showed a C5-C6 disc herniation.

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:17.240
Psychosocial variance covers yellow flags like fear, low mood or no transportation.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:22.430
On our file, that is the crash-related anxiety behind his missed visits in April.

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:25.608
And systemic variance is delay in the system itself,

00:29:25.608 --> 00:29:28.980
such as waiting on an authorization for imaging or a referral.

00:29:31.530 --> 00:29:36.377
You are the router for information, and a break in any of these three loops can

00:29:36.377 --> 00:29:37.850
stall a case for months.

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:42.348
The clinical loop: the therapist's notes should line up with the orthopedist's

00:29:42.348 --> 00:29:42.720
orders.

00:29:43.070 --> 00:29:48.150
For John, Riverton Spine and Rehab's notes should match what Dr. Reyes is seeing.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:53.366
The legal loop: your status reports to Atty. Thorne translate medical terms into

00:29:53.366 --> 00:29:57.927
what John can't do in daily life, such as lifting at work or playing pickup

00:29:57.927 --> 00:30:01.820
basketball, because that is the evidence for pain and suffering.

00:30:02.170 --> 00:30:05.330
And the financial loop: keep the carriers current.

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:09.738
On our file, Harbor Point's P I P went to the emergency care,

00:30:09.738 --> 00:30:14.927
and its MedPay then paid the urgent care, orthopedic and M R I bills until its

00:30:14.927 --> 00:30:18.520
$5,000 was used up, so you track both ledgers closely.

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:25.670
Every item in your monitoring plan has to meet two tests in utilization review.

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:29.700
Reasonable: is this treatment common for this mechanism of injury?

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:34.142
Necessary: is there objective evidence, not just reported pain,

00:30:34.142 --> 00:30:36.090
that it is improving function?

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:38.480
Then the red flag rule.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:43.896
The slide uses the Oswestry Disability Index after twelve weeks of therapy as its

00:30:43.896 --> 00:30:45.710
example, and that is general.

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:49.965
On John's file, the same idea shows up when Dr. Reyes,

00:30:49.965 --> 00:30:55.968
at his March 27th follow-up, refers him on to pain management instead of continuing

00:30:55.968 --> 00:30:56.980
the same care.

00:30:57.330 --> 00:30:59.931
A proactive Case Manager looks for the next step,

00:30:59.931 --> 00:31:02.850
and doesn't let treatment run on when it isn't working.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:08.480
When you spot a red flag, use A-C-T.

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:13.026
Assess: is this a medical danger, like a fever or hardware failure,

00:31:13.026 --> 00:31:16.470
or a legal danger, like a gap in care or generic notes?

00:31:16.820 --> 00:31:20.220
Clarify: call the provider's office and ask directly.

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:23.970
Track: document the red flag and what you did about it.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:27.400
On John's file, A-C-T runs in April.

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:32.030
On April 8th, Riverton Spine and Rehab sent a missed-visit alert.

00:31:32.380 --> 00:31:37.049
The Case Manager reviewed the file, then called and texted John the same morning,

00:31:37.049 --> 00:31:38.260
logging each attempt.

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:42.907
If the attorney ever has to make a decision about the case or a provider,

00:31:42.907 --> 00:31:44.850
your record is the reason he can.

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:50.480
This flowchart puts the treatment map on one page.

00:31:50.830 --> 00:31:56.604
Phase one: early diagnostics, establishing causation and the mechanism of injury,

00:31:56.604 --> 00:31:58.030
and verifying P I P.

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:03.879
On John's file, that is the EMS run report noting neck stiffness on the day of the

00:32:03.879 --> 00:32:07.500
crash, and Harbor Point's P I P, opened February 24th.

00:32:07.850 --> 00:32:10.410
Phase two: conservative care and imaging.

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:13.680
Then the key question: is the patient improving?

00:32:14.030 --> 00:32:17.230
If not, the variance loop begins, with injections,

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:20.430
a specialist referral and psychological screening.

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:25.380
John's file went through all three: Crescent, pain management and counseling.

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:29.958
Phase three is restorative, and phase four is maximum medical improvement and

00:32:29.958 --> 00:32:34.462
discharge, where the permanent impairment rating and the final lien resolution set

00:32:34.462 --> 00:32:35.450
up the settlement.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:40.600
Here is how to use the map for course correction.

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:43.990
First, find the plateau with the six-week rule.

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:47.902
Adjusters look for a functional change around week six,

00:32:47.902 --> 00:32:52.500
typically a 20 to 30 percent gain in range of motion or a drop in pain.

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:57.267
If the client is still at 10 out of 10 pain after twelve sessions,

00:32:57.267 --> 00:32:59.610
pivot now; don't wait for a denial.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:05.599
On John's file, his week-six re-evaluation at Riverton Spine and Rehab was scheduled

00:33:05.599 --> 00:33:09.560
for April 7th, and he missed it, which is itself a warning.

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:14.759
Second, measure with objective tools: a disability score every 30 days,

00:33:14.759 --> 00:33:19.744
gait analysis for leg injuries, the PHQ-9 and GAD-7 when progress stalls,

00:33:19.744 --> 00:33:23.910
and a functional capacity evaluation before a return to work.

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:31.632
Watch for yellow flags, the psychosocial barriers that knock a client off the map:

00:33:31.632 --> 00:33:36.740
catastrophizing, litigation stress, and dependence on passive care or medication.

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:41.130
On John's file, the yellow flag was crash-related anxiety.

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:44.929
After April 2nd he avoided riding in cars to appointments,

00:33:44.929 --> 00:33:49.746
and Dr. Reyes documented anxiety and avoidance on April 20th and referred him for

00:33:49.746 --> 00:33:50.400
counseling.

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:55.190
When you report to the attorney or a carrier, use the map to justify the plan.

00:33:55.540 --> 00:34:00.940
The slide's example, recommending an MRI and a neurosurgical consult for radicular

00:34:00.940 --> 00:34:02.060
pain, is general.

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:06.450
For John, the MRI was ordered by Dr. Reyes on March 5th.

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:09.440
That keeps the treatment reasonable and necessary.

00:34:11.990 --> 00:34:15.445
Treatment gaps weaken the credibility of the injury,

00:34:15.445 --> 00:34:20.230
slow recovery, create inconsistent records, and reduce settlement value.

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:26.108
Step in early, because a gap explained now is far easier than one explained at the

00:34:26.108 --> 00:34:26.580
demand.

00:34:26.930 --> 00:34:30.717
The common causes are missed appointments, transportation,

00:34:30.717 --> 00:34:35.027
cost and co-pays, work schedules, stopping after temporary relief,

00:34:35.027 --> 00:34:37.770
and delays in referrals or authorizations.

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:40.320
John's file has two gaps.

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:45.847
The first is fourteen days, from the crash on February 6th to his first treatment on

00:34:45.847 --> 00:34:46.710
February 20th.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:50.739
The second is nineteen days of disrupted care in April,

00:34:50.739 --> 00:34:54.820
from his visit on April 2nd until care resumed on April 21st.

00:34:57.370 --> 00:35:00.330
Your responsibilities with treatment gaps are concrete.

00:35:00.680 --> 00:35:02.960
Monitor frequency and attendance.

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:05.910
Follow up immediately after a missed visit.

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:09.260
Explain to the client why steady treatment matters.

00:35:09.610 --> 00:35:12.610
Help with transportation, scheduling and referrals.

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:15.520
Talk with providers about interruptions.

00:35:15.870 --> 00:35:18.950
And document every explanation and every follow-up.

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:22.676
As best practice, keep the treatment timelines current,

00:35:22.676 --> 00:35:27.340
and escalate a long or unexplained gap to supervising staff or the attorney.

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:30.250
On John's file, that work is visible.

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:33.988
After the April missed-visit alert came the calls,

00:35:33.988 --> 00:35:38.121
texts, an email, a call to Margaret as the emergency contact,

00:35:38.121 --> 00:35:40.560
and then escalation to Atty. Thorne.

00:35:40.910 --> 00:35:44.090
John called back on April 17th, and with his consent,

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:47.150
evening therapy visits were booked from April 21st.

00:35:47.500 --> 00:35:50.620
That record is what later protects the value of the case.

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:56.690
When a bottleneck stalls care, escalate in three levels.

00:35:57.040 --> 00:36:00.997
Level one, clinical: contact the provider's office manager,

00:36:00.997 --> 00:36:04.485
on our file Tammy Greer at Riverton Spine and Rehab,

00:36:04.485 --> 00:36:08.040
to move notes along or ask for a peer-to-peer review.

00:36:08.390 --> 00:36:12.804
Level two, administrative: send a notice of delay to the adjuster and the

00:36:12.804 --> 00:36:16.070
plaintiff's attorney, documenting the barrier to care.

00:36:16.420 --> 00:36:19.583
Level three, legal: ask the attorney about a motion,

00:36:19.583 --> 00:36:23.780
or about using a Letter of Protection to get past insurance stalling.

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:26.930
That decision always belongs to the attorney.

00:36:27.280 --> 00:36:31.721
And not every LOP request is accepted: when Summit Imaging asked for one,

00:36:31.721 --> 00:36:34.520
it was declined, because MedPay applied first.

00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:40.950
When the map hits a wall, climb the ladder one step at a time.

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:46.676
Step A, the clinician: ask for a peer-to-peer review between the treating doctor and

00:36:46.676 --> 00:36:48.660
the insurer's medical director.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:53.292
Step B, the adjuster: send a status update that says plainly that the delay is

00:36:53.292 --> 00:36:56.970
raising the total claim cost by extending the period of disability.

00:36:57.320 --> 00:37:01.057
Step C, the attorney: if step B fails, tell the attorney,

00:37:01.057 --> 00:37:06.040
who may send a 30-day demand or move the provider to a Letter of Protection.

00:37:06.390 --> 00:37:10.110
On John's file, Atty. Thorne is the one who makes that call.

00:37:10.460 --> 00:37:13.900
Your job is to bring him a clean record of each step you took.

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:18.210
That completes Day 1.

00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:23.107
You've seen what a Case Manager owns, how to run a clean intake and acceptance

00:37:23.107 --> 00:37:26.080
decision, how to set up the claim and its coverage,

00:37:26.080 --> 00:37:28.120
and how to keep treatment on track.

00:37:28.470 --> 00:37:33.410
You've also seen John Doe versus Robert Smith from the first call to maximum medical

00:37:33.410 --> 00:37:36.350
improvement, with its two gaps clearly documented.

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:40.580
Next, we take that file into the pre-demand audit and the demand.

00:37:40.930 --> 00:37:44.330
Thank you for your attention, and bring your questions to the next session.

